<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Carlism</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1833">1833</a>–</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Carlism is a Spanish traditionalist and legitimist political movement that arose in 1833 in support of Infante Carlos María Isidro's claim to the throne against his niece Isabella II. Strong in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a>, the Basque Country and Catalonia, it defended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> and regional fueros under the motto 'God, Fatherland, Fueros, King'. It fought three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlist-wars">Carlist Wars</a> and backed Franco in the Civil War.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/carlism.jpg" alt="Carlism" width="320" height="179" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hstoops · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Spanish_Conservatism_Flags_Symbol.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Carlism aimed to place on the Spanish throne a branch of the Bourbon dynasty descended from Don Carlos, Count of Molina, who lived from 1788 to 1855. It grew out of an early 19th-century dispute over the succession and out of dissatisfaction with the Alfonsine line of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">House of Bourbon</a>. Spanish kingdoms had traditionally allowed daughters to inherit in the absence of sons, but when the French Bourbon Philip V acceded on 1 November 1700, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salic-law">Salic law</a> of the French royal house led to a semi-Salic system excluding women unless all male descendants of Philip had died out.</p><p>Carlists defended that rule. The term carlistas for followers of Carlos María Isidro emerged in the mid-1820s, and the supporters themselves cautiously began to accept the name in the 1840s.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-carlist-war">First Carlist War</a>, from 1833 to 1840, was fought between supporters of the regent Maria Christina, acting for Isabella II, and those of the late king's brother Carlos de Borbón, whose goal was a return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/absolute-monarchy">absolute monarchy</a>. Portugal, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the United Kingdom supported the regency and sent volunteers and even regular forces. A minor Catalan uprising from 1847 to 1849 made up the Second Carlist War, and a third war began in 1872.</p><p>After the fall of Isabella II in 1868 and the strong support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-ix">Pope Pius IX</a>, many conservative Catholics joined the movement, and it had about 90 members of parliament in 1871. After its defeat in 1876 it returned to peaceful politics, building under the Marquis de Cerralbo a mass party around local assemblies called Círculos.</p><p>In July 1936 the Carlists joined the Nationalist rising uneasily. Their militia, the Requetés, took over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamplona">Pamplona</a> on 19 July, and in a few days almost all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> was occupied. Under Franco the movement was gradually marginalised. In the first democratic elections of 1977 only one Carlist senator was elected, and Carlists have since remained outside parliament.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The motto Dios, Patria, Fueros, Rey, meaning God, Fatherland, Local Rule and King, summed up its principles: Catholic faith as the basis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, patriotism seen as a nesting of communities, regional self-rule under fueros, and a king limited by the Church's doctrine and the laws and usages of the kingdom. Its flag was the red cross of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> on white, and the red beret was its emblem.</p><p>Cultural and political regionalism in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> is described as largely Carlist in origin, and Sabino Arana, a founder of Basque nationalism, came from a Carlist background. Writers including Ramón María del Valle-Inclán, a Carlist himself, wrote novels about it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Carlism” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Carlists</b> organised a real administration from Estella: ministries, courts, a postal service with its own stamps, coinage and a university at Oñati.</blockquote><a href="/state/carlist-state">Carlist State</a></li></ul></section>
